(54, $t$Common App personal essay not required. Instead, 4 Ut Prosim Profile short answers required of all applicants (120 words each): community contribution, experiences of inclusion/exclusion, leadership/pride experiences, etc. Cannot be edited after submission. Official page is titled '2026-2027 Short Answer Questions' — the 4-question, 120-word format is the same as the previous cycle.$t$, null, $t$Beyond Boundaries Scholars; Presidential Scholarship Initiative (PSI); VT Scholars$t$),
(55, $t$Required: "Why Wake?" (100 words max, supplement section). For additional short answers, respond to at most 1 of 4 prompts (list of 5 books you found interesting / intellectual curiosity / response to a Maya Angelou quote / open-topic Top 10 list). 'Sharing your voice' in writing or video is fully optional (explicitly stated as not required).$t$, $t$Starting in 2026-27, short-answer prompts changed from multiple responses to choosing 1 of 4, and Why Wake? (100 words) moved to the supplement questions section — change announced on the official admissions blog (2026.7).$t$, $t$Nancy Susan Reynolds Scholarship; Guy T. Carswell Scholarship; Joseph G. Gordon Scholarship; Stamps Scholarship; Graylyn Scholarship; Leadership and Character Scholarship; Presidential Scholarship for Distinguished Achievement$t$),
(56, $t$No required supplemental essay. Up to 2 of 6 optional short-answer prompts may be submitted (a community important to you, academic interests/career, family/background, what sparked your interest in W&M, a challenge you faced, a tour of your neighborhood, etc.; ~300 words each). The school strongly encourages submitting up to 2.$t$, null, $t$1693 Scholars Program; James Monroe Scholars Program; Provost Scholar Awards (out-of-state first-years); William & Mary Scholars Awards — all first-year applicants automatically considered (wm.edu/admission/financialaid/types/scholarships/merit/)$t$),
