(68, $t$2 required supplemental essays: ① Why CMC (tied to the mission) ② Open Academy (an experience where a conversation with someone of a different perspective changed your attitudes/beliefs). Separate optional 2-minute video response.$t$, null, $t$Official international student aid page: "CMC is need-aware in its admission process for all international students (non-U.S. citizens and non-U.S. permanent residents)... However, if admitted, CMC is committed to meeting demonstrated need as determined at the time of admission." / "Merit-based scholarships are available for all first-year applicants and are determined by the Office of Admission through the application review process." Apply by 12/1 for priority merit consideration. Scholarships page: McKenna Scholars ($15,000), Seaver Scholars (full-tuition). CDS H6: both need-based and non-need-based aid offered to nonresidents. Financial aid packages may include loans ("grants, scholarships, work, and loans").$t$),
(69, $t$2 required: Academic Interest Statement (150 words) + Short-Response Essay (choose 1 of 3 prompts, 250 words). As of 2026-08, the official page reflects Fall 2026 (not yet updated for 2026-27).$t$, $t$In the 2025-26 cycle (Fall 2026), reduced from 3 essays (150+150+250 words) to 2 (150+250 words); 2026-27 prompts not yet published on the official page.$t$, $t$Official: no academic or athletic scholarships (need-based only). Need-aware review for international students, but 100% of need is met for admitted students ("Admissions is need-aware for international applicants, but we meet the full need of every admitted international student"). Aid packages do not include loans ("Pomona does not use loans to meet a student's financial need").$t$),
(70, $t$1 required Wellesley-specific essay: 'build bridges' prompt (an experience working with people of different backgrounds/perspectives) — separate from the Common App personal essay (250-650 words).$t$, null, $t$Official: no merit scholarships, academic, athletic, or otherwise ("We do not offer academic, athletic or other scholarships"). Not need-blind for international students (non-citizens/non-permanent residents) ("Wellesley is not need-blind for students who are not U.S. citizens or permanent residents"); a limited number of need-based awards; 100% of calculated need met for admitted students ("committed to meeting 100% of calculated need for all admitted international citizens who have applied for financial aid during the admission process").$t$),
(71, $t$No supplemental essay (Common App main essay only). The Carleton Supplement form includes only personal information, how-you-heard-about-us questions, and an optional free-response field; no essay prompts.$t$, null, $t$Official: no merit scholarships ("We do not offer merit scholarships"; "All Carleton grants and scholarships are need-based"). Financial aid funding for international students is limited ("We have limited funding available to international students", "We offer a few need-based scholarships to international students"), and new aid applications are not accepted after admission. 100% of need met for all students ("we meet 100% of demonstrated need for every student"). Loans are included in small amounts ("We keep loans small and work-study manageable").$t$),
(72, $t$2 required additional writings: ① how your experiences/community shaped 'the problems you want to solve and the impact you want to make' (up to 500 words) ② an experience of asking for help to create change (up to 250 words).$t$, $t$In 2026-27, both required prompts were fully replaced — the previous mission/background prompt (500 words) + 'dream HSA class' (100 words) became a new experience/impact prompt (500 words) + 'asking for help' essay (250 words), with the second prompt's length expanded from 100 to 250 words.$t$, $t$Official: need-aware for international students ("Because funding for international students is limited, your level of financial need may affect your chances for admission if you apply for financial aid"). Meeting 100% of need is possible for only a very small number of international students ("We are able to meet 100% of a family's demonstrated need for only a handful of international students"). International students may also be eligible for some merit awards, and the So Scholarship is exclusively for international students. CDS 2025-26 H6: both need-based and non-need-based checked as 'offered'. Loans are included in packages (CDS H1 student loans, Private alternative loans).$t$),
(73, $t$2 required short answers: ① 'bold question' (up to 50 words) ② a lesson you would learn from a woman whose perspective and problem-solving approach differ from yours but who excels at bringing people together (200-250 words).$t$, $t$Reduced from 3 questions (200+150+150 words) to 1 question (200-250 words) in 2025-26, then reorganized into 2 required questions in 2026-27 with the addition of a 50-word 'bold question'.$t$, $t$Official: no merit scholarships ("Barnard gives no merit scholarships"). Need-aware for international students (non-citizens/non-permanent residents) ("We are need-aware for non-U.S. citizens and non-permanent residents"), with only a small number of need-based awards per entering class. 100% of need met for admitted students ("Barnard will meet 100% of the financial need of admitted students"). Aid packages combine grants, loans, and jobs (loans included).$t$),
(74, $t$Personal essay (250-650 words) + 2 required supplemental essays — ① Why Davidson (250-300 words) ② a topic/activity/idea that interests you (250-300 words).$t$, null, $t$Official: 'First-year applicants, including both domestic and international students, can be considered for Davidson's non-need-based scholarships.' Financial aid packages do not include loans (The Davidson Trust).$t$),
(75, $t$No required supplement — Common App essay only ('no required supplement' officially stated). If you wish, additional materials (writing samples, essays, etc.) can be attached via the portal after submitting.$t$, null, $t$International students' need is met through 'a combination of grants and student employment' (no loans). Official: 'Grinnell College has a need-aware admission policy for international students'.$t$),
(76, $t$No required supplement — 1 optional supplemental short answer (can be completed on any platform or in the applicant portal after submitting) + optional materials such as a Hamilton Hello video or a personal URL.$t$, null, $t$No merit scholarships (official FAQ: 'Hamilton discontinued offering merit scholarships with the class that entered in the Fall of 2008'). Need-based packages include small loans (up to $3,500 in the first year).$t$),
(77, $t$No supplement — officially states that no supplemental essay is required; submit only the Common App/Coalition personal essay (art portfolio optional, via SlideRoom).$t$, null, $t$No merit scholarships (official: 'All of Middlebury's financial aid is need based. We don't offer merit aid'). International students' need is met with 'a combination of work, loans, and grants'.$t$),
(78, $t$No supplement — submit only 1 Common App/Coalition essay (school-specific supplemental questions remain eliminated; arts supplement optional).$t$, null, $t$Official: 'Smith has eliminated loans from its undergraduate financial aid packages for those students receiving institutional need-based aid, replacing those funds with institutional grants.' International students must apply for financial aid by the deadline before admission (if they do not, they are permanently ineligible afterward).$t$),
(79, null, null, $t$Financial aid is entirely need-based ("Financial aid is awarded exclusively on the basis of need") and there are no merit scholarships. International students must apply for financial aid at the same time as applying for admission, and the admissions office is need-aware when reviewing international students.$t$),
(80, $t$No supplement — Common App/Coalition personal essay only; no school-specific additional essays (only optional portfolio/supplementary materials may be submitted).$t$, null, $t$"We do not include loans in our undergraduate financial aid packages." International students must apply for financial aid at the time they apply for admission (if they do not, they are ineligible for aid for all four years), and aid applicants who are citizens of 11 Asian countries, including Korea, are considered for the Freeman Asian Scholarship Program (4-year full scholarship). However, it explicitly states "admission for international students seeking financial aid is extremely competitive".$t$),
(81, $t$No required supplement — optional short-answer statement (respond to a short essay prompt in the applicant portal in writing or with a 2-minute video). 1 essay of up to 800 words required only for Johnson Scholarship applicants (deadline 2026-12-01).$t$, null, $t$"W&L admits students without considering their ability to pay" — need-blind for domestic, international, and undocumented students alike. "W&L meets the full demonstrated financial need of every admitted applicant entirely through grants and student employment, without loans." Merit aid is consolidated into the Johnson Scholarship (up to 44 students; full tuition + room + board), which international students may also apply for. W&L Promise: households with income under $200,000 receive at least a full-tuition grant. However, the international student page includes the statement "Admission to Washington and Lee does not guarantee financial assistance".$t$),
(82, $t$No required supplement — optional supplemental prompts (up to 250 words each, e.g., what you gain from engaging with a diverse community / what inspires you). Officially states there is no penalty for not submitting.$t$, null, $t$"Colgate does not offer academic merit scholarships" — entirely need-based. International student page: "We consider financial need during the review process for all applicants" (need-aware), "Colgate meets 100% of demonstrated financial need for every admitted student." The Colgate Commitment (for household income of $300,000 or less) is limited to U.S. citizens and permanent residents, and whether international students' packages include loans could not be confirmed on the official pages.$t$),
(83, $t$1 required supplement — Richmond Question (choose 1 of the given prompts, 650 words max). The format (1 required, 650 words) stays the same; only the prompt wording is updated each year — 2026-27 prompts officially released.$t$, null, $t$"The University of Richmond is committed to meeting the full demonstrated financial need of all admitted domestic and international applicants." International students are reviewed need-aware ("the Office of Admission is need-aware when reviewing applications from non-U.S. citizens and non-U.S. permanent residents"), and "International students... may receive full consideration for all merit-based scholarships, with the exception of the Army ROTC scholarship program" — Richmond Scholars consideration is automatic when applying by 12/1. International students must submit a Certification of Financial Responsibility.$t$),
(84, $t$No supplement — Common App personal essay only; no additional essay item on the official checklist (arts/creative supplement optional; supplement eliminated since the 2017-18 cycle).$t$, null, $t$Bates officially states that its financial aid is entirely need-based and that it does not offer merit scholarships to international students. It officially states that it meets 100% of calculated need for non-U.S.-citizen applicants without loans (grant + on-campus work), and that it is not need-blind (need-aware).$t$),
(85, $t$No supplemental essay — no additional writing required beyond the Common App/Coalition/QuestBridge personal essay. Only optional materials such as the Arts Supplement and InitialView may be submitted.$t$, null, $t$Colby Commitment: officially states that it meets 100% of calculated need for all admitted students (including international students) without loans (grant + on-campus work). No merit scholarships (academic, athletic, or other talent-based) (only a $2,000 National Merit Finalist recognition award). The admissions FAQ officially states 'Colby is need aware'.$t$),
(86, $t$2 required supplements — ① a topic that sparks your intellectual curiosity and how you would explore it at Haverford (150-200 words) ② the community values represented by the Honor Code and the values you are looking for (150-200 words).$t$, null, $t$All financial aid is need-based (no merit). Officially states that it provides financial aid to a limited number of international students each year and that students who receive aid have their full calculated need met. International students who do not receive aid in their first year cannot receive it in later years either.$t$),
(87, $t$No school-specific supplemental essay — the official application checklist (application, transcript, 2 recommendations, application fee) has no additional essay item. Only supplementary materials such as evidence of special talents may be optionally submitted.$t$, null, $t$The financial aid page officially states that it meets 100% of calculated need for all admitted international students, while the international student page states that funding for international students is limited (limited funding). There are a few merit scholarships, but international eligibility is not specified. Packages include federal and institutional loans (not no-loan). Households with income of $100,000 or less (with typical assets) receive a full-tuition grant.$t$),
(88, $t$2 optional essays — in the Common App member section: ① how the Twin Cities location benefits your academic/social/community experience ② how you connect with the 4 core values (academic excellence, internationalism, multiculturalism, service to society). Neither is required.$t$, null, $t$The international student FAQ officially states that financial need plays a role in admission decisions (need-aware), and also that it meets the full calculated need of every enrolling student. Merit for international students: Charles J. Turck Presidential Honor Scholarship ($15,000-35,000 per year, automatic consideration). Intent to apply for aid must be indicated at the time of application; aid cannot be requested after admission.$t$),
(89, $t$1 optional writing supplement — listed on the official application process page as 'Mount Holyoke writing supplement (optional)'. Common App/Coalition personal essay required separately.$t$, null, $t$Official international student page: 45% of new international students receive need-based scholarships (average USD 58,686) or merit scholarships. International students' need-based packages consist of grants + fixed-rate student loans + on-campus work, and the loan portion is stated to increase by at least $1,000 each year.$t$),
(90, $t$1 required writing supplement (250-500 words, submitted via the Common App) — 'Bryn Mawr students bring their authentic selves to campus...' prompt. At the time of research, the official page was labeled 2025-26 (prompt unchanged since 2024-25).$t$, null, $t$Official international student page: "International students are not eligible for merit aid." Need-based aid is available, but the page states "Bryn Mawr is a need-sensitive institution, meaning that while we meet the full calculated need of all admitted students, we have a limited amount of funds available". New financial aid applications not accepted after admission (must apply when applying for admission).$t$),
(91, $t$No long-form supplemental essay — only 1 school-specific question: a short answer (up to 250 words) explaining why you chose your first-choice major (or undecided) and second-choice major.$t$, null, $t$Official international financial aid page: "Bucknell is currently not 'need-blind' for international students", "Bucknell financial aid for international students is limited", "only a small number of highly qualified international applicants are ultimately chosen to receive financial assistance". International students who do not receive aid in their first year receive none in later years. International eligibility is not specified for the merit programs (Presidential Fellowship $30,000, Dean's Scholarship $20,000, Engineering/Management/Malesardi Merit Scholars $40,000, etc.).$t$),
(92, $t$1 optional supplemental essay (300 words max) — Block Plan-style prompt on 'an experience of deeply immersing yourself in one thing'. The official page (marked 2026-27) states there is no penalty for not submitting.$t$, null, $t$Official: "CC meets 100% of every student's demonstrated financial need" / "Colorado College meets the full demonstrated need for all students". International student page: "International applicants seeking financial aid comprise our most competitive group of applicants each year"; only a handful out of thousands can receive full aid. International students not applying for aid must demonstrate the ability to pay the full cost for 8 semesters. New financial aid applications not accepted after admission.$t$),
(93, $t$1 optional 'Why Lafayette?' essay (20-200 words) — on the Common App member page; not required.$t$, null, $t$Official: "Lafayette is proud to be one of only a select few colleges in the United States to guarantee to meet full demonstrated financial need of all admitted students, both domestic and international." However, "Lafayette College is need aware and considers a student's financial situation when making admission decisions." International students who do not receive aid in their first year cannot receive it later (budget constraints). Merit scholarships are decided by the admissions office without a separate application.$t$),
(94, $t$No supplemental essay — the official application page states 'No Supplemental Essay Required'. Submit only the Common App/Coalition personal essay.$t$, null, $t$Official page: "International students are eligible for the same institutional need-based financial aid and merit-based scholarships as domestic students"; merit scholarships range from $5,000 per year to full tuition. However, "Denison is need-aware in the application process" (needing aid affects admission competitiveness).$t$),
(95, $t$No supplemental essay — no additional essay item on the official application checklist. Only supplemental materials such as a portfolio may be optionally submitted.$t$, null, $t$International student page: "If you're admitted to F&M, we will meet 100% of your institutionally determined financial need for all four years" / "If you require a high level of aid, the admission process becomes more competitive" (need-aware). The financial aid page mentions 'Merit & Dedicated Scholarships (determined by the Office of Admission)', but individual names and international eligibility are not stated.$t$),
(96, $t$1 optional short answer (250 words max) — 'Why Occidental / why Oxy is the right place for you' prompt. The official checklist (Fall 2027 entry = 2026-27 cycle) states 'not mandatory'.$t$, null, $t$International student page: "Need-based financial aid for international students is very limited and highly competitive" / "Occidental typically offers need-based aid to only a small number of international applicants". The cost & financial aid page states '100% demonstrated financial need met' (for all students overall). The official site mentions a minimum $15,000-per-year scholarship for ED admits.$t$),
(97, $t$1 required writing supplement (650 words max) — choose 1 of 2 prompts (① your engagement with one of the 5 core values ② the college experience you want and your fit with Pitzer).$t$, null, $t$Financial aid page: "We meet 100% of students' demonstrated need." Merit scholarships listed are the Trustee Community Scholarship ($5,000 per year, renewable) and Academic Achievement Scholarship ($2,000 one-time), but international eligibility is not stated. International students apply for need-based aid through the PIFAA (Pitzer International Financial Aid Application) (no CSS Profile/FAFSA submitted).$t$),
(98, null, null, $t$International financial aid page: "A limited amount of financial aid is available to international students" / "Scripps College is need aware in our admission process" / financial aid is determined only at the time of admission. Main financial aid page: "Scripps meets 100 percent of a student's institutionally determined financial need." International eligibility for merit scholarships is not stated.$t$),
(99, $t$No separate supplemental essay — only 1 essay required. If applying via Apply IU, an IU-specific prompt (200-400 words, your academic/career plans and the activities you want to do at IU); if applying via the Common App, one of the Common App essay prompts may be used instead.$t$, null, $t$International first-year applicants who apply by the Nov 1 early action deadline are automatically considered for IU academic scholarships (no separate application; amounts not published). Named international awards also exist (C.V. Starr, Rising Star, etc.) plus the one-time $7,500 Global Citizen Scholarship (separate video application).$t$),
(100, $t$No MSU-specific supplemental essay. 1 essay required (250-650 words) — choose from 7 prompts (including an open topic); this corresponds to the Common App personal essay. Used to improve admission chances and in scholarship review.$t$, null, $t$MSU states that all international students admitted to MSU are automatically considered for its non-resident/international scholarships and that no application beyond the application for admission is required; published award levels are President's $15,000, Provost's $12,000, Dean's $10,000, 1855 Scholarship $7,000, Beaumont Tower $5,000 and Global Community Scholars $3,000 per year. Applying by the November 1 Early Action deadline is required for maximum scholarship consideration, and MSU notes that meeting the criteria does not guarantee an award.$t$),
(101, $t$Common App/Coalition essay (650 words or fewer) required + 1 required NC State short answer — 'why you chose the major(s) selected above and why you want to study at NC State' (word count not listed on the official page; 250 words max per guidance). University Honors Program applicants add an essay of 600 words or fewer (optional, 11/1 deadline, same prompt as Park Scholarships).$t$, $t$The second short answer asking why you chose your second-choice major was removed, leaving 1 short answer on why you chose your major.$t$, $t$NC State states that international applicants are not eligible for financial aid, but that a limited number of competitive merit-based scholarships are available to international students. Admitted students access the PACK ASSIST scholarship portal and must submit the general scholarship application; the Park Scholarship is restricted to U.S. citizens, permanent residents, or graduates of a U.S. high school.$t$),
(102, $t$No supplemental essay for general applicants — the general essay/personal statement in the application meets the essay requirement ('satisfies our requirements'). However, applicants to Architecture, Electronic Arts, Games and Simulation Arts and Sciences, Music, and Physician-Scientist (B.S./M.D.) must submit program-specific essays (Common App writing supplement).$t$, null, $t$RPI states that it offers a limited number of merit-based scholarship opportunities for international undergraduate students, including awards of up to $20,000 per year, and that additional institutional funding may be available to recipients of the Rensselaer Medal Scholarship or Division I athletic scholarships. The Rensselaer Medal is awarded through partner high schools worldwide to students distinguished in mathematics and science and is worth $40,000 per year, guaranteed for four years (five for the B.Arch.). Apart from Canadian citizens, who may be considered for an institutional need-based grant, RPI advises international students to plan to cover the full cost of attendance.$t$),
(103, null, null, $t$UMass Amherst states that international undergraduate students are considered for merit scholarships by the Admissions Office as part of their admission to the university, and that first-year applicants are automatically considered with no separate application; awards are communicated in the acceptance letter and renew automatically with full-time enrollment and satisfactory academic progress. It also states that international students are not eligible for most types of financial aid because the FAFSA is limited to U.S. citizens and eligible non-citizens, and that additional scholarships may be available through a student's school, college or major.$t$),
(104, $t$No supplemental essay — official announcement: 'The University of Miami does not require a supplemental essay for first-year admission as of the 2026-2027 application cycle.' Submit only the Common App personal essay (required). Some programs such as the Frost School of Music require separate application materials (auditions, etc.).$t$, $t$Starting in 2026-27, Miami's own supplemental essay prompt was dropped; only the Common App personal essay is required.$t$, $t$Miami states that all students who apply are automatically considered for its merit scholarships regardless of citizenship status and that no additional scholarship application is required, with the largest awards going to those who apply by the November 1 early deadlines; standard awards are the President's Scholarship (up to $30,000 per year) and the Canes Achievement Award (up to $20,000 per year). Its premier awards open to all incoming first-year students are the Stamps Scholarship (full cost of attendance plus a $12,000 enrichment fund) and the Isaac Bashevis Singer Scholarship (full tuition), while the Hammond and Weeks scholarships are limited to U.S. citizens and permanent residents.$t$),
(105, null, null, $t$"The Wien Scholarship is awarded to exceptional international applicants who demonstrate strong academic achievement and extracurricular or community involvement" and "meets the full demonstrated financial need of each recipient"; there is no separate application - all candidates offered admission are considered. Brandeis also administers the Davis United World College Scholars Program ($10,000 grant for UWC graduates with financial need) and the Toshizo Watanabe International Scholarship for students graduating from a Japanese high school.$t$),
(106, $t$No supplemental essay — the items listed in the official Application Instructions are only the application (including activities list and personal statement), transcript, recommendations, and optional test scores; there is no additional essay such as 'Why Tulane'. The Common App personal essay (personal statement) is required.$t$, $t$The optional 'Why Tulane' supplemental essay was discontinued (dropped) starting with the 2026-27 cycle; only the personal essay is submitted.$t$, $t$"All applicants are considered for partial merit scholarship by completing the Common Application," and Tulane says its merit awards "may cover your full tuition and fees or may even cover your full cost of attendance"; "Tulane's Global Scholarships are renewable for 4 years (5 years for students in the 5-year Architecture program)." Separately, "Tulane offers up to $30,000 in need-based financial aid to international students," which requires the CSS Profile (recommended by February 15).$t$),
(107, $t$No supplemental essay. Common App/Coalition personal essay required (recommended range 250-650 words) — officially stated as 'The personal essay is required'. Only nursing (DeLuca School of Nursing) applicants answer 1 additional question on their motivation for nursing.$t$, null, $t$UConn lists the Global Excellence Award, Global Distinction Award and Global Leader Award as merit-based scholarships "available to international applicants; all campuses," renewable for four years at a fixed amount set yearly. First-year applicants are automatically considered for most merit scholarships on initial review, except the Nutmeg, Day of Pride and Stamps Scholarships, which require a supplemental application.$t$),
(108, $t$No supplemental essay. An essay is not required for admission itself — the official required items are only the application, fee, STARS (self-reported grades), and optional test scores. However, a personal statement is needed to be considered for scholarships ('required to be considered for scholarships'), so submitting one is effectively recommended. Frederick Honors College is reviewed separately.$t$, null, $t$"International first-year applicants who apply by February 1 are automatically considered for merit-based scholarships" of up to $10,000 per year, typically available for eight full-time undergraduate terms and renewable with Satisfactory Academic Progress; no separate application is required, but the international short answer, Common Application essay or a personal statement must be submitted and awards apply only to fall (August) entry.$t$),
(109, $t$No supplement. No school-specific supplemental essay required beyond the main essay in the Common App/SUNY App/Coalition application (no essay item on the official checklist).$t$, null, null),
(110, $t$No supplement. An essay is not an admission requirement at all; all additional materials such as a personal statement are optional ('Clemson does not require any of these additional materials for admission').$t$, null, null),
(111, $t$No supplement. 1 main essay required — Rutgers' own application: 3,800 characters (incl. spaces); Common App: up to 650 words. Officially states there is no supplemental essay for the Common App route; a short statement is added only when applying for Honors.$t$, null, null),
(112, $t$No supplement. No writing supplement item on the official checklist (Common App main essay only). However, Architecture and Visual and Performing Arts require additional college-specific materials such as a portfolio/audition.$t$, null, $t$Syracuse's financial aid office states, "All students who apply for admission to Syracuse University are considered for merit-based scholarships, regardless of citizenship or country of birth," and that international undergraduates are considered for merit scholarships based on their application for admission. No separate scholarship application is required.$t$),
(113, $t$No supplement. No school-specific supplemental essay required beyond the Common App/Coalition main essay (official requirements: transcript and major selection; recommendations and test scores optional).$t$, null, $t$UB states it "offers scholarships in varying amounts to qualified incoming international first-year/first-time students" and that "All admitted international students are automatically considered for scholarships during the holistic review process," renewable for up to four years if continuing eligibility requirements are met. UB advises applying by November 1 for priority scholarship consideration.$t$),
(114, $t$UC common application Personal Insight Questions: respond to 4 of 8, up to 350 words each. No campus-specific additional essays.$t$, null, $t$UCR states that first-year nonresident students "may receive up to $89,100 for four years" and that international students are automatically considered upon acceptance once confirmed as nonresidents and meeting UC eligibility requirements, with a 3.0 GPA required to keep the award while at UCR. No separate scholarship application is required.$t$),
(115, $t$No supplement. The essay is an optional, not required, submission (considered only as optional material in holistic review).$t$, null, $t$Mines states that "International First-Year Students are eligible for up to $8,000 in an admissions award"; the application is evaluated for merit aid by the Undergraduate Admissions Office and the award is renewable for 8 consecutive semesters toward tuition and fees. Students with an outside sponsorship may not be eligible, and the Financial Aid Office states that international students are generally not eligible for (need-based) financial aid.$t$),
(116, $t$No supplement. 1 Common App/Coalition (Scoir) main essay of 250–650 words required; no Drexel-specific supplemental essay.$t$, null, $t$Drexel states that "Students admitted through Early Decision are eligible for a minimum of $20,000 in merit scholarships along with consideration for need-based aid," and its international admissions page states that "Drexel does offer need-based aid to qualifying international students." Merit awards are made by the Admissions Committee on the basis of academic performance with no separate application; per CDS H6, 1,038 nonresident undergraduates received institutional aid averaging $32,937 in 2024-2025.$t$),
(117, $t$No supplement. Only the Common App main essay is used in evaluation; no NJIT-specific supplemental essay required.$t$, null, $t$NJIT states that "Although international students aren't eligible for federal aid, there are plenty of other opportunities for financial assistance," and points international students to NJIT scholarships. Merit awards require no separate application: "Once you are admitted as a first-time, full-time freshman, your academic records such as SAT/ACT scores and high school GPA, are reviewed to determine your eligibility for NJIT scholarships," and most require full-time enrollment and a 3.0 GPA to renew. No international-specific award name or dollar amount is published officially.$t$),
(118, $t$No supplement. 1 main essay included in the application ('Essay — included on your application') required; no Stevens-specific supplemental essay. Portfolio strongly recommended for Music and Visual Arts & Technology applicants.$t$, null, $t$Stevens states that "Stevens awards a limited number of merit scholarships to international students" and that it "may offer a merit based Global Scholarship to international students with exceptional academic achievement." International students who want to be considered for institutional scholarships must file the CSS Profile (school code 2819); no dollar amounts are published.$t$),
(119, $t$1 required supplement: why you are applying to Pepperdine, a Christian university, and how you would contribute to faith conversations on campus (300–500 words). Submitted in addition to the Common App main essay.$t$, null, $t$All admitted students are automatically considered for the Blanche E. Seaver International Scholars' Awards; no separate application is required, and the merit-based award is offered to a limited number of candidates. Recipients must maintain a 3.25 Pepperdine cumulative GPA and full-time status; Pepperdine also names the Charles Lam Scholarship ($8,000-$10,000/yr, citizens of Mainland China) and the Lydia Hayne Scholarship Fund (full tuition, room and board, citizens of an African country, offered once every four years).$t$),
(120, $t$Common App main essay required + 1 required short essay in the UIC Member Profile (on your intended major or career field of interest). Word count not listed on the official page.$t$, null, null),
(121, $t$No supplement. The official application requirements (Common App, transcript, recommendations) include no WPI-specific supplemental essay.$t$, null, $t$WPI offers competitive academic merit-based scholarships to first-year and transfer applicants based on academic performance, leadership experiences, extracurricular involvement, and community service; all applicants for admission are automatically considered and no additional application is required. IB diploma graduates with a diploma score of 40 or greater admitted as first-year students are guaranteed a merit scholarship worth a minimum of $25,000, and starting with students entering in fall 2026 all students admitted under an Early Decision plan are offered a minimum merit scholarship of $25,000 per year for up to four years.$t$),
(122, $t$Per YU's own application: 1 main essay (choose from 7 prompts) + 1 Personal Statement required (how YU will support your academic goals and help you grow personally and in your values, 250 words max). Interview required for all applicants. The official page lists prompts for 'Spring 2027 admissions and beyond'.$t$, null, $t$Yeshiva University's international admissions page states that YU offers merit-based scholarships to international students, and that although U.S. federal and state financial aid is not available to non-citizens, students may also be considered for institutional need-based aid; an international aid application is added to the applicant portal after applying. No named international merit program is published on the official admissions pages.$t$),
(123, $t$1 optional supplement: 'American University students identify as changemakers... Describe a belief, hobby, idea, issue, or topic about which you're excited' (2026-2027 cycle specified; word count not listed on the checklist page). Main essay required.$t$, null, $t$AU states that qualified international first-year applicants are automatically considered, with no separate application, for partial merit scholarships of $8,000 to $25,000 per year, renewable for four years. The separately-applied AU Emerging Global Leader Scholarship covers all billable expenses for two students, with up to eight additional partial awards of up to $40,000 per year. AU states that no need-based financial aid is available to international students.$t$),
(124, $t$1 main essay required (goBAYLOR or Common App). The Short Answer Response ('Why Baylor / contribution', ~450 words) is required only for 11/1 Early Decision (binding) applicants; recommended (optional) for all other applicants.$t$, null, $t$Admitted first-time international students are automatically considered for merit-based scholarships based on a complete review of their high school academic performance. For need-based consideration Baylor requires the CSS Profile (code 6032) by the November 1 priority deadline or the February 1 regular deadline; beginning with Fall 2027 a new Baylor International Financial Aid Application (BIFAA) opens October 1 with a November 1 priority and February 1 final deadline.$t$),
(125, $t$Common App main essay required + 1 optional essay (accessed in the Common App Writing section; no obligation to submit).$t$, null, $t$Howard states that international applicants must submit the HU ISFAA to be considered for financial aid, and that applicants who do not submit test scores receive equal consideration for scholarships based on other academic achievements. Applying by the deadline of the chosen admission plan is required for priority scholarship consideration. No international-specific named merit program is published on the admission site.$t$),
(126, $t$No supplement. 1 main essay (personal statement) required (Common App or Marquette's own application). Additional personal statements and recommendations not required (reviewed only if submitted).$t$, null, $t$All admitted international undergraduates are automatically considered for Marquette merit scholarships, including the Père Marquette Award, at the time of admission; some additional merit scholarships require a separate application after admission. Marquette states that it is not able to award need-based scholarships to international students.$t$),
(127, $t$No supplement. 1 Common App/RIT application main essay required. 1 additional supplemental essay required only for Honors Program or Individualized Program BS applicants.$t$, null, $t$RIT states that first-year merit scholarships range from $14,000 to $30,000 annually ($56,000 to $120,000 over the degree) and that all applicants, including international applicants, are considered for merit scholarships just by applying, with awards announced at the time of admission. RIT states that full-cost or full-tuition scholarships are not typically offered to undergraduates and that only very limited need-based funding is available; total support must reach at least $78,000 USD for RIT to issue the I-20.$t$),
(128, $t$No required supplement. Main essay of 250–650 words required on the application platform (Common App, ApplyTexas, SMU's own application, Coalition); the official requirements page lists no SMU-specific required supplemental essay.$t$, null, $t$All students who apply to SMU, regardless of citizenship, are automatically considered for SMU's academic merit scholarships with no separate application. Automatic consideration requires applying by November 1 (Early Decision I / Early Action) or January 15 (Early Decision II / Regular Decision); need-based financial aid is available only to U.S. citizens and permanent residents.$t$),
(129, $t$UC common application Personal Insight Questions: respond to 4 of 8, up to 350 words each. No campus-specific additional essays.$t$, null, $t$Newly admitted out-of-state and international first-year students are automatically considered for the Undergraduate Dean's Award during the admissions cycle; first-year awards range from $12,000 to $100,000 paid evenly over four years ($3,000-$25,000 per year). International students are not eligible for federal, state, or university need-based aid.$t$),
(130, $t$No supplement — submit only the Common App/Coalition personal essay (required); no additional essays.$t$, null, $t$All first-year international applicants are automatically considered for UD merit scholarships of $5,000-$15,000 per year, with no separate scholarship application. International students may also compete for UD's Distinguished Scholars awards, which can cover tuition plus room and board for four years if a 3.0 GPA is maintained.$t$),
(131, $t$No essay — only the application (USF's own or Common App) + fee + SSAR self-reported grades + test scores required; no personal essay or supplement (separate application only for Judy Genshaft Honors College).$t$, null, $t$Admitted international students are automatically considered, with no separate application, for the Green and Gold Presidential (about $44,000 total, up to $11,000 per year), Directors (about $36,000, up to $9,000 per year), and Scholars (about $20,000, up to $5,000 per year) waiver awards. These are partial out-of-state tuition waivers, require completing the freshman application by the January 15 scholarship deadline, and cannot be combined with another tuition-waiver scholarship.$t$),
(132, $t$No essay — only the Common App application, test scores (SAT/ACT/CLT), and transcript required; no personal essay or supplement (personal statement only for Honors College applicants).$t$, null, $t$FIU states it automatically considers admitted first-time-in-college students for merit-based scholarships when they submit and complete the freshman admissions application by the posted date (December 3, 2025 for students attending out-of-state or overseas high schools for Early Fall/Fall 2026), including official SAT, ACT or CLT scores. Eligible students must be U.S. citizens, lawful permanent residents, or international students receiving an F-1 (or another eligible) visa; no additional application is required.$t$),
(133, $t$No required supplement — Common App essay (required) + 3 optional short responses (Unique Fordham question, Additional information, Challenges and circumstances). Listed as 'Optional application components' on the official page, as of the 2026-27 deadlines.$t$, null, $t$Fordham states that all complete applications for admission are automatically considered for merit scholarships, that no supplementary materials are required, and that merit award notifications are included within the online admission letter. Its International Student Scholarships page lists competitive four-year awards, including the full-tuition Presidential Scholarship for entering first-year students who generally rank in the top 1-2 percent of their high school class.$t$),
(134, $t$No supplement — Rutgers' own application requires 1 essay (up to 3,800 characters; choose 1 of 7 prompts or an open topic); the Common App requires only the personal essay (250-650 words). Officially states there is no supplemental essay when applying via the Common App (only a short statement added if being considered for Honors).$t$, null, $t$Rutgers–Camden states it will offer scholarships of up to $21,000 to all non-sponsored international applicants who meet admission requirements and enroll full time, with no separate application required and automatic consideration on admission. The award is renewable if the student maintains a 2.5 GPA at Rutgers–Camden, and international students are encouraged to apply by the early action or regular decision due dates because scholarship funds are limited.$t$),
(135, $t$1 essay required (300-500 words) — TCU's own application: choose 1 of 4 prompts; Common App, Coalition, and ApplyTexas use that platform's essay (no separate TCU supplement). An optional 'Freedom of Expression' submission (essay, poem, artwork, URL) is recommended.$t$, null, $t$TCU states that two types of assistance are available to incoming international students — academic scholarships and need-based financial aid — and that applicants are automatically considered for academic scholarships when they apply for admission. Need-based awards range from $2,000 to $41,570 and require the College Board CSS/Financial Aid PROFILE (TCU code 6820) by February 1 for first-year fall applicants; TCU notes it does not offer full-funding awards.$t$),
(136, $t$Common App personal essay (250-650 words) + 1 required supplemental short answer (250 words, 'what do you want to study at CU Boulder and why' — must differ from your personal essay). Officially states that applications without essays cannot be reviewed. Confirmed on the 2026-27 cycle page.$t$, null, $t$CU Boulder states that international students are eligible for a few merit-based CU Boulder scholarships and that the university cannot provide federal financial aid to international students; it does not accept the College Board International Student Financial Aid Application. Athletic and College of Music scholarships (full or partial) are occasionally available to very talented individuals.$t$),
(137, null, null, $t$All admitted first-year applicants, including international students, are automatically considered for merit scholarships with no separate application; awards for high school applicants are based on GPA and portfolio.$t$),
(138, null, null, $t$All admitted Parsons applicants, including international students, are automatically considered for merit scholarships based on the strength of the application and portfolio; no separate application is required. International students are typically eligible only for merit scholarships.$t$),
(139, null, null, $t$RISD states that institutional funding is awarded based on financial need at the time of admission; international applicants are considered by completing the RISD institutional financial aid form in the applicant portal. RISD notes it should not be assumed that aid offers will meet full financial need.$t$),
(140, null, null, $t$International students are eligible for select institutional scholarships, typically based on artistic merit as judged from the portfolio, audition and/or writing sample; all applicants are automatically considered with no separate application. CalArts states these funds are limited and competitive and do not cover a student's entire tuition.$t$),
(141, null, null, $t$Cooper Union awards every enrolled undergraduate, domestic and international and in all three schools (art, architecture, engineering), a half-tuition scholarship, currently valued at $22,275 per year. It does not offer separate academic merit scholarships; additional aid is need-based, and international students are not eligible for federal or state aid.$t$),
(142, null, null, $t$International undergraduates are reviewed for SAIC merit scholarships during the admissions process, based on the portfolio, artist's statement and other materials, with no separate application. The Early Action/Priority Merit deadline gives the widest access to merit awards; international students are not eligible for U.S. federal aid.$t$),
(143, null, null, $t$International students are eligible for the merit-based Silas H. Rhodes Scholarship (first-time freshmen, full-time, minimum 3.0 GPA); there is no separate application, and freshman materials must be submitted by February 1. The Gustave Blache III Art Scholarship (partial tuition, December 1 deadline) is also open to international first-time freshmen.$t$),
(144, null, null, $t$FIT's financial aid office states that international students are not eligible for financial assistance from FIT; they may only borrow through the Alternative (private) Loan Program, usually with a creditworthy U.S. co-signer. F-1 visa holders are not eligible for the New York State resident tuition rate and pay the out-of-state rate.$t$),
(145, null, null, $t$MICA's merit-based competitive scholarships recognize artistic talent and academic achievement and are open to students from the United States and abroad; new applicants are considered through the admission application. International students may also submit the CSS Profile to be considered for need-based aid.$t$),
(146, null, null, $t$Incoming undergraduates, including international students, are automatically reviewed for GPA-based academic scholarships after admission, and those who submit a résumé/list of achievements and/or portfolio are also reviewed for achievement scholarships. The International Student Scholarship requires meeting or exceeding admission requirements and a 500-word essay.$t$),
(147, null, null, $t$International undergraduates may be considered for merit-based scholarships through the admission application but are not eligible for federal aid; MassArt Provost's Scholarships are merit-based awards for accepted first-year and transfer applicants from outside New England, renewable for up to four years. Test scores are not used for merit scholarship consideration.$t$)
) as v(id, essay_req_en, essay_change_en, merit_note_en)
where s.id = v.id;

-- Harvey Mudd 장학금 설명에 섞여 있던 내부 필드 문구 제거
update public.schools set merit_note = regexp_replace(merit_note, '\s*→\s*meets_full_need_intl false\.?', '.') where id = 72;

-- 국제학생 장학금 이름(merit_intl) 영어판 — 한국어가 섞인 11곳 직역, 나머지는 원문(영어) 복사 (2026-09-24)
alter table public.schools add column if not exists merit_intl_en text;
update public.schools set merit_intl_en = merit_intl where merit_intl is not null and merit_intl !~ '[가-힣]';
-- 11곳 번역문은 docs/i18n-review/school-essay-merit-en.md 하단 표 참고 (DB 반영 완료)
